# Seeds /var/lib/dnsmasq/dns-server.conf the FIRST time the router boots
# with that file missing (systemd.tmpfiles 'C' rule in modules/dhcp-dns.nix
# only copies when the destination doesn't already exist) - i.e. the
# baseline restored on a full reimage. This deliberately seeds Pi-hole
# (10.0.0.53), the steady-state post-cutover value, not dnsmasq's own
# 10.0.0.1 - see scripts/dns-failover.sh.
#
# Cold-start gap this creates: on a genuinely fresh reimage, the Pi-hole
# container image (~500MB) still has to be pulled before Pi-hole is
# actually answering DNS on 10.0.0.53 - any DHCP client that grabs a
# lease in that window keeps a dead resolver for up to 12h (the lease
# time). Low-frequency (only a fresh reimage, not normal operation/
# reboots, since the image is already pulled after that), so this is
# documented rather than architecturally solved. If LAN DNS seems dead
# right after a fresh install, run `sudo dns-failover bypass` on the
# router for the immediate fix (falls back to dnsmasq itself, unfiltered)
# and switch back to `dns-failover pihole` once the image has finished
# pulling.
dhcp-option=option:dns-server,10.0.0.53
